      "contested": "GEM records Capacity=87.00 MMcf/d. The 2000 FERC FEIS/R Federal Register notice for the original conversion project states the pipeline 'would be capable of transporting up to 80 to 90 million cubic feet per day (MMcfd) to customers east of California and at least 120 MMcfd to customers in southern California' -- 87 MMcf/d falls squarely inside the 80-90 MMcfd 'east of California' range, which is a good match given the current system no longer serves California. A separate NGI article cites 80,000 Dth/d for part of the historic East Zone flow, which is a different (smaller, likely partial or a specific contract-flow) figure and not necessarily inconsistent (Dth/d and MMcf/d are near-equivalent units at typical heating values, and 80,000 Dth/d =~ 78-80 MMcf/d, close to but not identical to 87).",
